> > > > > > > > > > > the
> > > > > > > > > > > BYM, i.e. model jointly (Y, U), extending the framework of
> > > > > > > > > > > Riebler et
> > > > > > > > > > > al.
> > > > > > > > > > > (2016) to the multivariate case (three-variate in this
> > > > > > > > > > > case).
> > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > However, I get strange results for the correlation matrix
> > > > > > > > > > > of the spatial
> > > > > > > > > > > effects, e.g. the correlation between the 1st and 3rd BYM
> > > > > > > > > > > field results
> > > > > > > > > > > about -0.92 in mean. This does not appear consistent with
> > > > > > > > > > > the data
> > > > > > > > > > > (trivially speaking, observations are not negatively
> > > > > > > > > > > correlated).
> > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > More 'familiar' models like the M-model extension of the
> > > > > > > > > > > Leroux CAR give
> > > > > > > > > > > indeed different results. The LCAR is closely based both
> > > > > > > > > > > in terms of
> > > > > > > > > > > prior
> > > > > > > > > > > choice and coding on the bigDM package, which implements
> > > > > > > > > > > some M-models
> > > > > > > > > > > in
> > > > > > > > > > > INLA and is documented in a paper by Vicente et al. (2023)
> > > > > > > > > > >
> > > > > > > > > > > In both cases (LCAR and BYM) I use a Uniform prior on the
> > > > > > > > > > > mixing
> > > > > > > > > > > parameter
